  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Tracing the \"403\" Error**\n\nOkay, I\'ve dived into the \"403 Forbidden\" error, focusing on line 551 of `server.ts`. It seems there\'s a user ID check that\'s failing. I need to understand why `memorialOwnerWithFallback.user_id` isn\'t matching `user.id`. Let\'s step back and follow the data flow.\n\n\n**Investigating Memorial Ownership**\n\nI\'m now focusing on memorial ownership discrepancies related to the 403 error. Specifically, the checks on lines 551 and 784 are in question, particularly with `nascimentopalotina@gmail.com`. It appears the error stems from a mismatch between the user ID and the memorial owner\'s ID during narrative API calls. I suspect the user is trying to edit a SaaS memorial, which is returning the 403 error. I will verify if this user\'s memorials are from the SaaS list.\n\n\n**Analyzing Test Failure Details**\n\nI\'m now investigating a test failure related to memorial generation. I see that the `test_real_generation.ts` test, specifically concerning memorial ID `e0cd7ab2-4e0b-4c33-893e-8a6eca7120ad`, is failing due to an unexpected database query error. The error indicates that the `memoriais.edits_used` column is missing, even though I believe the test was returning status 200 before, due to a hardcoded response. I need to understand why this column is now absent.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the \"403\" Issue**\n\nI\'ve made a breakthrough. The mocked user ID used in testing, \'d5ffac32-29c6-48d3-8ba2-37c42338fd7f\', matched the memorial owner\'s ID for `e0cd7ab2-4e0b-4c33-893e-8a6eca7120ad`. But in the UI, we have a real user, `nascimentopalotina@gmail.com` (ID `78654881-7776-4391-8e3f-03d54bc135d9`). The real user got a 403 Forbidden. Now, I need to investigate the server logs to understand if the UI user is requesting a forbidden action.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 6
      nanos: 86584177
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
