    thinking: "**Investigating Server Setup**\n\nI\'ve made a key discovery. The `vercel.json` file directs to `/api/index.ts`, but the development environment uses `server.ts` to run Express on port 3000. It seems the sandbox and deployment methods differ in their approaches. I\'m now examining `server.ts` for more details.\n\n\n**Analyzing Access Denial**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a \"403 Forbidden\" error. Could the user be accessing the app via a shared or development URL with an unauthorized Supabase session? I\'m re-examining `server.ts` to understand token rejection. My plan involves enhanced logging in `server.ts` to capture the request URL, method, user ID from the `Authorization` token (partially masked), and the `memorial_id` and owner\'s `user_id` when a 403 occurs, to help isolate the problem.\n\n\n**Examining Token Authentication**\n\nI\'m now revisiting the `authenticateToken` function in `server.ts`. It\'s essential to understand the token flow. I\'m focusing on the `401` status which represents a missing token, and how that interacts with Supabase, specifically, the usage of both `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL` and `VITE_SUPABASE_URL`, potentially leading to confusion if not consistently managed. I am now looking at the conditions which would produce a `401` error.\n\n\n**Debugging Token Verification**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed a critical issue. The `server.ts` file doesn\'t log the error returned by `supabase.auth.getUser(token)` when the token is invalid. Currently, it simply returns a 403 error. By adding a `console.error(error)` call, I can uncover the precise reason for authentication failures (invalid token, expired token, etc.). This logging oversight is a serious issue that makes debugging very difficult. I\'m now implementing this logging within the token verification process, hoping for useful output!\n\n\n**Improving Error Clarity**\n\nI\'m now focused on refining error messaging. Currently, 403 errors offer little insight. I\'ve realized that adding detailed error messages within the 403 response body allows the client to display specific reasons, enhancing the debugging process significantly. I\'m exploring adding an error endpoint that offers detailed debugging info, and examining every instance where a 403 status is returned in `server.ts` to improve clarity.\n\n\n**Refining Error Responses**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the `/api/narrativas/generate` endpoint, especially the validation logic. I\'ve found an oversight: the 404 response doesn\'t provide enough details about *why* the memorial might be missing. I\'m expanding this to include the `ownerError` details, if present. I\'m also scrutinizing the fallback value for `edits_used` to ensure its correct integration.\n\n\n**Validating User Access**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the `/api/narrativas/adjust` endpoint. It has similar validation logic to the generate endpoint. I\'m scrutinizing how the owner verification works, especially the handling of potential `ownerError` messages and owner mismatches. I want to make sure the error messages and details are comprehensive. I will examine how the access denial is handled, making sure the user gets a helpful error message with specifics.\n\n\n"
