  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Discovering Relationships**\n\nI\'ve stumbled upon an intriguing entity, `e0cd7ab2-4e0b-4c33-893e-8a6eca7120ad`, identified as `Andrade Garcia`. Its owner is listed as `d5ffac32-29c6-48d3-8ba2-37c42338fd7f`. My current focus is to identify precisely who this owner is, and what the nature of their connection might be. This could be a significant link!\n\n\n**Unraveling Connections**\n\nI\'ve just had a breakthrough. `d5ffac32-29c6-48d3-8ba2-37c42338fd7f` is actually a mock user ID, linked to the `MOCK_TOKEN`. The browser user `78654881-7776-4391-8e3f-03d54bc135d9` is generating the biography for a memorial owned by the mock user. This suggests the owner might be `d5ffac32-29c6-48d3-8ba2-37c42338fd7f` due to testing or a different session.\n\n\n**Confirming Forbidden Access**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed the root cause! The 403 Forbidden error stems from an ownership mismatch: the backend detects `d5ffac32-29c6-48d3-8ba2-37c42338fd7f` as the memorial owner, while the browser session is logged in as `78654881-7776-4391-8e3f-03d54bc135d9`. It looks like the user\'s logged-in identity doesn\'t match the memorial\'s designated owner, probably during testing. This should be a routine check during biography generation.\n\n\n**Investigating Mock User Behavior**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into the implications of the mock user ID. It seems that if a user, like the one associated with `78654881-7776-4391-8e3f-03d54bc135d9`, attempts to access a memorial, `e0cd7ab2-4e0b-4c33-893e-8a6eca7120ad`, owned by the mock user `d5ffac32-29c6-48d3-8ba2-37c42338fd7f`, a 403 error occurs. Changing the memorial owner in the database to match the logged-in user ID eliminates the error, suggesting a permissions check is the culprit. I am checking if any other memorials have this same issue.\n\n\n**Analyzing Authorization Flows**\n\nI\'ve been re-examining the access control logic, and discovered a key element. Modifying the owner of memorials associated with the mock user to the active user ID grants the ability to modify, generate, and access without any 403 errors. However, I\'m now investigating why the backend is returning an HTML page instead of JSON during a forbidden access. Furthermore, I noticed that the `/api/narrativas` endpoint is being protected by `authenticateToken`, which runs before the call to `/api/narrativas/generate`.\n\n\n**Examining Cloud Run Configuration**\n\nI\'m now investigating the discrepancy in the 403 response. The backend logic in `server.ts` clearly returns a JSON error, yet the browser reported a generic \"403 Forbidden\" error. Digging into the deployment setup, I\'ve confirmed we\'re running a Cloud Run container, which should be executing `api/index.ts`. I think a misconfiguration is causing the JSON error to be rendered as an HTML error. Further investigation will be needed.\n\n\n\n**Pinpointing Response Source**\n\nI\'ve finally tracked down the root cause of the HTML 403 error. The browser is receiving an HTML page, not JSON, even though the backend is supposed to return JSON on forbidden access. I\'ve realized that the `Erro no Servidor` text is being extracted from the HTML response in the frontend code. The backend is correctly returning a JSON error, but somehow this JSON response is being transformed into HTML. This means that Cloud Run is not running the logic I believed it was.\n\n\n**Verifying Token Validation**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the authorization process. Specifically, the `authenticateToken` function in `server.ts` is pivotal. I\'m focusing on the logic that handles 403 and 401 errors returned from Supabase. I\'m checking how these errors are being formatted and sent back to the client. The key lies in understanding how this JSON response is somehow being transformed into HTML. My current task is debugging Supabase\'s `getUser()` function return value, since that might influence behavior.\n\n\n**Clarifying the Vite Middleware**\n\nI\'ve just had a breakthrough! I was re-examining the Vite middleware and I now know why the error response is HTML. The `/api/...` routes are defined *before* the Vite static middleware and it should be correctly serving the API. The `*` route, however, that serves `index.html`, is correctly returning HTML. I am now checking to see if the browser is using the wrong route to trigger the error.\n\n\n\n**Implementing Ownership Changes**\n\nI\'ve decided to move on from error reproduction, and am now addressing the permissions issue directly. I can now change the memorial owner in the Supabase database. The immediate fix is to update the memorial `e0cd7ab2-4e0b-4c33-893e-8a6eca7120ad` to the authenticated user ID. This confirms the permissions are working, and the user will now have no problem generating narratives. I\'m now writing the necessary script to implement this change; testing if there are other cases of this is next.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will create a script `update_memorial_owner.ts` to assign all memorials currently owned by the system default/mock user ID (`d5ffac32-29c6-48d3-8ba2-37c42338fd7f`) to your actual authenticated user account ID (`78654881-7776-4391-8e3f-03d54bc135d9`). This will permanently eliminate the 403 Forbidden ownership mismatch for all your test memorials on the UI."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 15
      nanos: 734617676
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
