  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Confirming Log Reception**\n\nI\'ve confirmed the `server_console.log` file is receiving logs successfully. Currently, it just shows the server running message: \"Server running on http://localhost:3000\". Now, I\'m thinking about how user requests to backend endpoints will be logged.\n\n\n**Analyzing Request Logging**\n\nOkay, I\'m now focusing on how user requests will be captured in `server_console.log`. I realize there are no current requests. To populate the log, I\'m considering two methods: running a test script to call the endpoints myself, or waiting to see if the user runs the app. I\'m also examining error handling in `/src/pages/saas/SaasGerarBiografia.tsx`, looking for clues on how failures might manifest in the logs. If they get an error, I wonder what the log will say?\n\n\n**Investigating HTTP 403 Errors**\n\nI\'m now investigating the source of the `403 Forbidden` error. I\'ve ruled out `authenticateToken` and server configuration. I believe Nginx, our reverse proxy, may be the culprit. I am focused on understanding the scenarios when Nginx returns this error, looking into potential blockages or rate limiting. I am considering the impact this may have on logging user requests, specifically how Nginx responses would appear.\n\n\n**Exploring Payload Size Limits**\n\nI\'m now considering payload size limits as a cause of the issue, realizing a \"Payload Too Large\" error might be more likely than a 403. However, I\'m still trying to understand why Nginx would return a `403 Forbidden` error. I\'m focusing on the request itself: Content-Type, missing headers, or a format triggering security rules in the Web Application Firewall (WAF) seem like possibilities. I\'m verifying if the request even reaches the Express server. I am also investigating error responses in Express, and if a 403 would ever render HTML.\n\n\n**Diagnosing Authentication Failure**\n\nI\'m now investigating a possible authentication failure. I\'m focusing on the `SaasGerarBiografia.tsx` component and the `fetch` call, realizing `session` and `session.access_token` might be undefined. This could explain why I\'m getting a 403, and the Express router is never even called, and I\'m looking at how the client handles an invalid token. Since the client is receiving a `403` response with HTML, something *explicitly* returned a 403, not an implicit fallback.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Incorrect Error Response**\n\nI\'ve just realized a critical inconsistency. While I initially suspected an `Authorization` header with a value of `Bearer undefined` would cause a `403` error, the actual `authenticateToken` middleware returns a `401 Unauthorized` instead if the token is undefined. I am now working through the flow to understand why the client is explicitly receiving a `403` response with HTML, if the token is handled by middleware. This means that a separate issue outside of token validation must be responsible for the 403.\n\n\n**Confirming Ownership Validation**\n\nI\'ve just pinpointed the root cause! The 403 error, explicitly returned as JSON due to an ownership mismatch, is correctly parsed on the client side, showing a user-friendly error message, such as \"Acesso negado: Voc\303\252 n\303\243o \303\251 o dono deste memorial.\" The client-side parse, shown in the previous update, is functioning correctly. I\'m satisfied.\n\n\n**Investigating Source of Error**\n\nI am now focusing on the exact error message reported: \"Erro no Servidor: 403 Forbidden\". The key is that this specific string indicates they are using the Development App Cloud Run URL, and therefore the server.ts backend. I\'m investigating the potential causes of a `403` status from the `/api/narrativas` endpoint within the Express environment, including Supabase RLS policies and how the backend handles Supabase authentication failures. I\'m checking if Supabase returning a 403 impacts the process of the Express backend.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will write a script to simulate a direct POST request to `http://localhost:3000/api/narrativas/generate` on the active dev server, so we can analyze the exact HTTP response (headers, raw body, content type) and see if the server itself is generating the 403 HTML page."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 11
      nanos: 128336903
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
