    thinking: "**Investigating Environment Variables**\n\nOkay, so I thought I had an environment variable issue, but the test script `test_env_vars.ts` showed `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL` as false and `VITE_SUPABASE_URL` as true. So, that\'s not the culprit. Now, I\'m re-examining the code in `server.ts` to see what I missed.\n\n\n**Analyzing Token Verification**\n\nOkay, I\'m shifting focus back to token verification. The 403 error suggests a problem with the token itself. Although `getUser(token)` relies on an anonymous key, the JWT verification process should still work if initialized with an anon_key. The earlier error, triggered by a test token, had an invalid format! I\'m now questioning if the issue originates within the API endpoint used.\n\n\n**Clarifying Request Handlers**\n\nI\'ve realized AI Studio doesn\'t use Vercel\'s serverless functions. My thoughts are shifting to Express and `server.ts`. It seems `api/index.ts` is not directly involved, as Express handles the routes. I am now looking at the `authenticateToken` middleware, focusing on the `/api/narrativas/generate` route. My concern is whether unhandled promise rejections are causing 500 errors misinterpreted by the frontend. I will check error message parsing in `SaasGerarBiografia.tsx`.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Potential 404s**\n\nI\'m now investigating potential 404 errors, specifically in relation to Supabase queries. My current hypothesis centers around `ownerError` in the `server.ts` file, and its impact on fetching memorial data. I\'m focusing on why a 404 might occur, even if the memorial ID exists. The key query is failing to find data. I\'m examining how that might be, since the database dump shows the memorial is present. It could be permissions?\n\n\n"
